-- title:
--   Truncated multivariate power series: free of rank prod Nᵢ
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a nontrivial commutative ring, let $g$ be a natural number and let $N : \mathrm{Fin}\,g \to \mathbb{N}$ be a family of natural numbers. Consider the ring $R[[X_i : i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,g]]$ of multivariate formal power series in $g$ variables over $R$, and let $I$ be the ideal generated by the set of powers $X_i^{N_i}$, $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,g$ (that is, the span of the range of the map $i \mapsto X_i^{N_i}$). The assertion is the conjunction of three statements about the quotient $R[[X_1,\dots,X_g]]/I$ regarded as an $R$-module: it is a free $R$-module; it is a finitely generated $R$-module; and its rank over $R$ equals $\prod_{i} N_i$. Note that no positivity is assumed for the exponents $N_i$: if some $N_i = 0$ then $I$ is the unit ideal, the quotient is the zero module and the product is $0$. The statement records the numerical rank only, not the explicit monomial basis indexed by the box $\{m : m_i < N_i \text{ for all } i\}$.
--
--   This is the elementary computation that a power series ring truncated by a monomial ideal of the form $(X_1^{N_1},\dots,X_g^{N_g})$ is a finite free module over the base, of rank the product of the exponents. It is used in the study of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where quotients of this shape occur as the coordinate rings of kernels of isogenies and their heights and ranks have to be computed.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_free_and_finite_and_finrank_quotient_span_range_X_pow.lean

